It's rather the effectiveness of the PR that is the focus of doubt.
Whatever is effective to win - to win possibly every game - is good.
So plans that reduce risk are better than plans that introduce risks.

Being so wimpy that you have to rely on your pet is a risk; your pet
will die and you stay alone with XL:1 and lousy HPs in a difficult
dungeon area, the mines. Entering the mines in the first place is a
decision for itself; dark mines are bad, getting surrounded in the
open place is bad, etc. If your alignment and/or class in specific
games produce peaceful gnomes and/or dwarfs the risk calculation may
lead to different results and the PR might get a viable tactics. If
you're confronted with a monster's wand attack, won't you retreat to
the main dungeon, e.g. to try to get the amulet in Sokoban? It's IMO
a point of criticism of its own to rely on a single tactics like PR.

My question to you is; if you can survive a risky tactics like the PR,
why do you need it at all to win? Honestly, to me this sounds strange.

Or the other way round; if you don't do the PR why do you die? Doesn't
your pet help you as it does in the mines? Don't you find enough armor
spread all around in the dungeons as others seem to to? Can't you gain
advantages of a "flexible response" in concrete situations, as opposed
to relying to a fixed tactics that also seems to need a lot training
(as TJR has mentioned)?

> [...]
> 
> My point is that if there is one winning strategy (and very likely
> there's one, as suggested by Marvin's record)

I just see a winning streak, not the presence of a fixed strategy. Is
there any? Is any of his ascensions based on the PR? If not; is the PR
completely devaluated and superfluous, then?

> it can only be improved
> by doing the PR first. To put it another way, if PR has a 100% success
> rate, then it is at the very least an improvement on any existing
> winning strategy.

If anything has a 100% success that is (at least) not bad as part of any
strategy. My experience is that in Nethack nothing guarantees an absolute
success. But you can always make a risk estimate, partly a static one,
and partly ad-hoc depending on the actual game. Claiming a value of 100%
isn't honest, though. If I'd make statements about success of ordinary
players at some stage I'd say - mind these are arbitrary numbers! - that,
if one has a success rate of 3% regularily he has a rate of 1% with PR.
or if an advanced player has a success rate of 80% he would have a 75%
rate with PR. This expressed tendency is based on what I wrote above
about specific risks with the necessary environment (mines, pets, etc.).

If you really have 100% (or so) success with PR, as you claim, then there
shouldn't be any reason to not have also a 100% (or better ;-) success
with a less risky and more adaptive strategy.

